WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.950
Wearable sensors for monitoring, health and injury prevention. D2.4 Implementation
Guide · Video 3 of 4 · Chapter 4.

2
00:00:06.000 --> 00:00:16.288
What wearable sensors measure. A GPS vest holds a small unit on the upper back: GPS
for distance and speed outdoors, motion sensors for accelerations and impacts, also

3
00:00:16.338 --> 00:00:18.950
indoors. A chest strap measures heart rate.

4
00:00:19.000 --> 00:00:28.485
Small-academy option. No sensors yet? Start with session-RPE: each player rates how
hard the session was from 0 to 10. Multiply by the minutes. Validated, and costs

5
00:00:28.535 --> 00:00:28.950
nothing.

6
00:00:29.000 --> 00:00:38.985
Before you start. Numbered GPS units, vests in the right sizes, a charging dock.
Heart-rate straps (optional). A laptop or tablet with the software and the player

7
00:00:39.035 --> 00:00:42.950
list. A data protection check: wearable data can be health data.

8
00:00:43.000 --> 00:00:45.950
Once, before the first session.

9
00:00:46.000 --> 00:00:53.950
Step 1. Choose a system and check its test report. FIFA's EPTS quality programme
lists tested providers with a public test report.

10
00:00:54.000 --> 00:01:03.538
Step 2. Give each player their own numbered unit. The same unit every session keeps
a player's data comparable over time. Check: The player list shows one unit per

11
00:01:03.588 --> 00:01:03.950
player.

12
00:01:04.000 --> 00:01:12.950
Step 3. Enter each player's measured maximum heart rate. From a field test agreed
with your medical staff. An age-based estimate can be wrong for an individual.

13
00:01:13.000 --> 00:01:18.950
Step 4. Write down your speed zones and keep them all season. Changing zones mid-
season breaks comparisons.

14
00:01:19.000 --> 00:01:25.950
Step 5. Record the unit model and firmware version in your log. A dated note
explains a sudden jump.

15
00:01:26.000 --> 00:01:28.950
Before every session.

16
00:01:29.000 --> 00:01:32.950
Step 6. Charge units and straps the day before. Follow your manual.

17
00:01:33.000 --> 00:01:40.950
Step 7. Create the session in the software and split it into drills. Same naming
pattern every time: date – age group – session type.

18
00:01:41.000 --> 00:01:51.804
Step 8. Fit the vest tightly so the unit sits on the upper back. A loose unit
bounces and adds false accelerations. Check: The unit does not move when the player

19
00:01:51.854 --> 00:01:52.950
jogs on the spot.

20
00:01:53.000 --> 00:01:59.950
Step 9. Switch units on outdoors and wait for satellite lock. Away from buildings.
Waiting times differ by brand.

21
00:02:00.000 --> 00:02:03.450
Figure 4.1  Session setup. 1  Name the session the same way every time.

22
00:02:03.500 --> 00:02:06.950
Figure 4.1  Session setup. 2  Split it into drills.

23
00:02:07.000 --> 00:02:10.450
Figure 4.1  Session setup. 3  One unit per player.

24
00:02:10.500 --> 00:02:13.950
Figure 4.1  Session setup. 4  Check signal and battery before you start.

25
00:02:14.000 --> 00:02:16.950
After every session.

26
00:02:17.000 --> 00:02:24.950
Step 10. Download the data and check signal quality. Mark sessions with poor signal
and do not compare them with normal sessions.

27
00:02:25.000 --> 00:02:29.950
Step 11. Label indoor sessions as "indoor". GPS distance and speed are not reliable
indoors.

28
00:02:30.000 --> 00:02:37.950
Step 12. Ask each player for a session-RPE rating. "How hard was the whole session?"
(0–10) × minutes. Example: 4 × 87 = 348 arbitrary units.

29
00:02:38.000 --> 00:02:44.950
Step 13. Put units back on charge and store the data safely. Only named staff should
see the data.

30
00:02:45.000 --> 00:02:52.735
Interpreting wearable data. Wearable numbers are estimates with a margin of error.
Compare like with like: not across brands or systems. Ask how a brand's own load

31
00:02:52.785 --> 00:03:00.472
score is calculated. Read youth data in light of age, growth stage and fitness.
Compare planned intensity with what players report. Do not treat numbers as injury

32
00:03:00.522 --> 00:03:00.950
forecasts.

33
00:03:01.000 --> 00:03:10.961
Watch out: the acute:chronic workload ratio. Do not use the ACWR as a red flag for
injury. Researchers found conceptual and statistical problems with it and

34
00:03:11.011 --> 00:03:12.950
recommended that it be dropped.

35
00:03:13.000 --> 00:03:23.165
In the training week. Same routine every time: charge, fit, lock, record, download,
check. A short report soon after the session: a few numbers and one clear message.

36
00:03:23.215 --> 00:03:24.950
One data owner, and a backup.

37
00:03:25.000 --> 00:03:33.075
Common problems. Distance and speed missing or far too low: Check the session
profile; switch on outdoors and wait for lock. Heart-rate strap shows no signal: Wet

38
00:03:33.125 --> 00:03:40.950
the electrodes, tighten the strap, pair again. Sudden change in all players'
numbers: Check your log for an update or changed zones. All problems: Table 4.1.

39
00:03:41.000 --> 00:03:50.130
Quick start by role. Coach: Plan intensity (easy, medium, hard) for each drill.
Scout: Never rank players on one physical number alone. Performance staff: Check

40
00:03:50.180 --> 00:03:54.950
signal quality after every session. Academy manager: Name a data owner and a backup.

41
00:03:55.000 --> 00:04:03.950
